  vk26-alice-3-the-count-the-estimate-rests-on-1.txt: J1 NO [README.md:207, README.md:208, README.md:209, README.md:210] The README says vk26-alice-5 found that "the RTL counts one more when the word after the halting `ecall` is a legal instruction". That is an RTL behaviour claim the bundle does not support: it has no alice-5 material, no theorem or capture about the word after the `ecall`, and the README only links out. The README at 460-461 and 443-446 also cites the XIP cache's size and ways, the branch predictor's cost and the cache-set offsets from outside sources. Capture line 230 gives only the three-boolean layout result for those offsets. Most other numeric board claims do match the captures, including the slow-run counts per stream and the probe results.
  vk26-alice-3-the-count-the-estimate-rests-on-2.txt: J1 NO [README.md:207, README.md:684] README.md:207 says vk26-alice-5 "later found the cause" of the extra RTL instruction, but nothing in the bundle holds that finding. README.md:684-685 says the same extra instruction "is not explained", so the two statements contradict each other. The rest of the checked claims match the proof files, capture.txt and the round logs; I could not confirm every figure from the truncated board-log slices.
  vk26-alice-3-the-count-the-estimate-rests-on-3.txt: J1 NO [README.md:207, README.md:208, README.md:209, README.md:210] README.md:207-210 says vk26-alice-5 "found the cause" of the extra instruction: the RTL counts one more when the word after the halting `ecall` is a legal instruction. The bundle holds no evidence for this finding, since alice-5 is not in it. Every other substantive claim I checked matches the proofs, `capture.txt`, or the recorded board logs. That covers the slow-run tallies (5 of 5, 4 of 6, 16 of 16, 2 of 3), the probe results, and the +3 minstret.
