  vk26-alice-5-the-seed-the-tests-passed-1.txt: J1 NO [README.md:426, README.md:427, README.md:436, README.md:440, README.md:411, capture.txt:247] The board counts do not add up. README.md:411 says 202 cases were judged right. README.md:426-427 split them into 104 with a 48-byte seed material and 99 with an 80-byte one, which sums to 203. capture.txt:247 lists 80-byte Instantiate timings (11372 in 78, plus the other values) that total 98. README.md:436 ("203 times") and README.md:440 ("all 203") also count 203 against the 202 whole cases reported in the log header and capture.
  vk26-alice-5-the-seed-the-tests-passed-2.txt: J1 NO [README.md:427, capture.txt:247] README.md:426-427 says 104 cases had a 48-byte seed material and 99 an 80-byte one (and "78 of 99", "203 times", "all 203" elsewhere). The board mcycle counts in capture.txt:247 sum to 104 and 98, which is 202 whole cases, and the 404 Generates on line 248 are also 2×202. The board-count claims therefore go beyond the evidence.
  vk26-alice-5-the-seed-the-tests-passed-3.txt: J1 NO [README.md:426, README.md:436, README.md:440, capture.txt:247, capture.txt:248] The README says 104 cases had a 48-byte seed material and 99 an 80-byte one (203 in all), 260 was seen "203 times", and "all 203" second Generates took 15,088 cycles. The board's page summary and the capture both say 202 cases. The capture's Instantiate counts are 62 of 104 and 78 of 98, not "78 of 99", and its Generate counts sum to 404 (2 × 202), not 406. The 15,088 count of 377 mixes first and second Generates, so the claim about every second Generate is not shown either. The bundle's board log is a slice, so the "28 of 83 slow in both rounds" and "clusters 16 cases apart" claims cannot be checked against it.
  vk26-alice-5-the-seed-the-tests-passed-1.txt: J2 NO [README.md:127, README.md:128, proof/Seed.lean:787, proof/Seed.lean:788, proof/Seed.lean:844, proof/Seed.lean:845, proof/Seed.lean:355, proof/Seed.lean:358] `inst_run`/`inst_boot` are of `from_boot` form and match the text (any seed material with S ≤ 0x80, any `env.hash`, halts with 0 after 241, `Laid`). `chain` is weaker than its description, though. The README and docstring describe the shell's step as "the pc at base, any registers", but `restart` is defined as `{ s with pc := base, mem := … }`, so it keeps the previous kernel's registers. `chain` therefore only states the Instantiate and Generate runs for that one register file, not for any registers the shell leaves.
  vk26-alice-5-the-seed-the-tests-passed-3.txt: J2 NO [README.md:127, README.md:128, README.md:129, proof/Seed.lean:787, proof/Seed.lean:788, proof/Seed.lean:844, proof/Seed.lean:845, proof/Seed.lean:355, proof/Seed.lean:358, proof/Seed.lean:767, proof/Seed.lean:771] `inst_run`/`inst_boot` are of `from_boot` form and match the README: any hash, any seed material length S ≤ 0x80, HALT 0 after exactly 241 instructions, the region `Laid`. But the README says the chain's shell step starts the next kernel with "any registers", while `restart` (`{ s with pc := base, mem := … }`) keeps exactly the registers the previous kernel left, so `chain` covers only that one register state for both the Instantiate and the Generate runs, a fixed input where the text says "any".
